            "<strong>Airway.</strong> An unconscious patient's tongue falls back and her secretions pool, so the first question is whether air is moving freely. <strong>Snoring</strong> means the tongue is obstructing; <strong>gurgling</strong> means secretions or vomit; <strong>stridor</strong> — a high-pitched noise on inspiration — means laryngospasm, oedema or, after neck surgery, a haematoma pressing on the trachea; <strong>silence with a heaving chest</strong> and no air at the mouth means complete obstruction and is the most urgent of all. The response is in order: <strong>chin lift or jaw thrust</strong>; <strong>suction</strong> of the mouth and pharynx under direct vision with a rigid catheter, briefly and only on withdrawal; an <strong>oropharyngeal airway</strong> sized from the incisors to the angle of the jaw, left in until the patient <strong>gags or pushes it out</strong>, which means her reflexes have returned; and the <strong>lateral (recovery) position</strong> for every unconscious patient whose operation allows it, so that the tongue falls forward and vomit drains out rather than in. The laryngeal mask or endotracheal tube is removed by the anaesthetist, and <strong>laryngospasm</strong> after removal is treated with jaw thrust and 100 % oxygen under positive pressure while help is called. Any patient who is not maintaining her own airway stays in recovery with a nurse's hands on her jaw until she does.",
            "<strong>Breathing.</strong> The nurse counts the <strong>respiratory rate</strong> and watches the depth, the symmetry of chest movement, the colour and the <strong>oxygen saturation</strong>, and gives <strong>oxygen</strong> by a simple face mask at 5–10 L/min (never below 5, or carbon dioxide is rebreathed) until the patient is awake and keeping a saturation of <strong>94–98 %</strong> on air, or <strong>88–92 %</strong> in the patient with chronic lung disease who depends on a low oxygen level to breathe. <strong>Hypoventilation</strong> — slow or shallow breathing, a rising carbon dioxide, drowsiness — has a short list of causes and each has a sign: <strong>opioids</strong> (a slow rate, pinpoint pupils, sedation — treated with stimulation, oxygen and <strong>naloxone 0.1–0.4 mg intravenously, titrated</strong> so that the pain relief is not all reversed, and repeated because naloxone wears off before morphine does); <strong>residual muscle relaxant</strong> (a weak, floppy patient who cannot lift her head off the pillow for five seconds, cannot squeeze a hand firmly, breathes in a jerky uncoordinated way and looks frightened — the anaesthetist is called to give more reversal and the patient is ventilated by bag and mask until it works); <strong>pain</strong> that splints the chest; obesity and a tight dressing; and <strong>atelectasis</strong> from shallow breathing. <strong>Hypoxia</strong> announces itself as <strong>restlessness, confusion, agitation and tachycardia</strong> long before cyanosis — a restless patient in recovery is hypoxic, in pain or has a full bladder until proved otherwise, and is never simply sedated.",
