            "<strong>Consciousness</strong> is recorded as a level — unrousable, rousable to voice, awake and orientated — and watched for its direction; <strong>pupils</strong> are checked after neurosurgery and head injury and when opioids are suspected; <strong>blood glucose</strong> is measured in every diabetic, every child and every patient who is slow to wake; and <strong>movement and sensation</strong> in the legs are recorded after a spinal or epidural and in the operated limb after orthopaedic and vascular surgery. <strong>Emergence delirium</strong> — thrashing, crying, pulling at lines — is common in children, after ketamine and in the elderly; the nurse protects the airway, the lines and the patient from falling, rules out <strong>hypoxia, pain and a full bladder</strong>, keeps the room quiet and a parent close, and sedates only on the anaesthetist's instruction once the dangerous causes are excluded.",
            "<strong>Pain</strong> is assessed as soon as the patient can answer — a score out of ten at rest and on movement, or a behavioural scale for the child and the patient who cannot speak — and treated before it is severe, because established pain needs more drug and causes shallow breathing, tachycardia, hypertension and misery. The recovery nurse <strong>titrates intravenous opioid</strong> (morphine in small doses every five minutes against the pain score, the sedation score and the respiratory rate), adds paracetamol and a non-steroidal where allowed, makes sure the local anaesthetic block or the epidural is working, starts the <strong>patient-controlled analgesia</strong> pump if one is prescribed, and uses position, warmth, a hand and an explanation alongside. Pain that is <strong>out of proportion</strong> to the operation, or in the wrong place, is reported — it may be compartment syndrome under a cast, a distended bladder, ischaemia or a surgical complication.",
