            "The ward nurse's care begins with the same assessment the recovery nurse made — <strong>airway, breathing, circulation, consciousness, pain, temperature, wound, drains, lines and catheter</strong> — before the recovery nurse leaves, and continues as a schedule that tightens or relaxes with the patient's condition. A common pattern after major surgery is observations <strong>every 15 minutes for the first hour, every 30 minutes for the next two, hourly for four hours and then four-hourly</strong> for 24 to 48 hours, more often after any change, with each set scored on the <strong>early warning chart</strong> so that the trend is visible and the escalation is automatic. The nurse <strong>reports</strong>, without waiting for the next round: a respiratory rate below 10 or above 24, a saturation below the target, a pulse above 100 or rising steadily, a systolic pressure below 90 or falling by more than 20 % from baseline, a temperature below 36 or above 38 °C, a urine output below 0.5 mL/kg/h (about 30 mL/h in an adult) for two hours, new drowsiness or confusion, pain not controlled by the prescription, a dressing soaked through, a drain filling with fresh blood, vomiting that persists, a distended abdomen or bladder, calf pain, or anything the patient says is wrong — and she records what she reported, to whom, at what time and what was done.",
            "<strong>Breathing</strong> deteriorates in the first days because pain, opioids, a tight abdomen and lying still make the patient breathe shallowly, collapse the small airways at the lung bases (<strong>atelectasis</strong>) and let secretions pool and infect. The prevention is taught before the operation and enforced after it: <strong>sitting up</strong> at 30–45 degrees as soon as the surgeon allows, <strong>deep breathing</strong> — ten slow deep breaths every hour while awake, the incentive spirometer where there is one — <strong>coughing with the wound supported</strong> by the hands or a pillow, <strong>turning</strong> two-hourly, <strong>getting out of bed on the first day</strong>, enough <strong>analgesia</strong> to make all of this possible, <strong>hydration</strong> and humidified oxygen so that secretions stay loose, mouth care, and chest physiotherapy for the patient with chronic lung disease, the smoker and the patient who had an upper abdominal or thoracic operation. A rising respiratory rate, a fever on the first or second day, crackles or reduced air entry at a base, a productive cough or a falling saturation mean atelectasis or early pneumonia and are reported.",
