          heading: "Pain: by the clock, by the ladder, by the patient",
          paragraphs: [
            "Postoperative pain is expected, it is treatable and it is the nurse's responsibility — a patient in pain breathes badly, will not cough or walk, sleeps badly, is tachycardic and hypertensive, develops ileus and clots, and remembers the hospital as a place of suffering. Analgesia is <strong>multimodal</strong>: regular <strong>paracetamol</strong> (1 g four times a day in an adult of normal weight, less in the small, the malnourished and the patient with liver disease), a <strong>non-steroidal anti-inflammatory</strong> such as diclofenac or ibuprofen where there is no kidney disease, asthma that reacts, peptic ulcer, bleeding risk or heart failure, an <strong>opioid</strong> — morphine by mouth, subcutaneously or intravenously, tramadol, or pethidine, which is still widely used in Ghana despite its short action and toxic metabolite — and <strong>local anaesthetic</strong> infiltrated in the wound, a nerve block or an epidural. For the first 24 to 48 hours after major surgery analgesia is given <strong>regularly, by the clock, not on request</strong>, because pain that is prevented needs less drug than pain that has returned, with <strong>breakthrough doses</strong> prescribed for movement, physiotherapy and dressing changes, and it steps down the ladder as the pain settles. Before every opioid dose the nurse records the <strong>pain score</strong> (at rest and on movement), the <strong>sedation score</strong> and the <strong>respiratory rate</strong>, and withholds the dose and calls if the patient is difficult to rouse or breathing fewer than about 10–12 times a minute; naloxone is on the ward. The <strong>side effects</strong> are anticipated: <strong>constipation</strong> is universal with opioids and a laxative is started with the first dose; <strong>nausea</strong> has an antiemetic prescribed alongside; <strong>urinary retention</strong>, itching and drowsiness are watched for. Children are dosed by weight, the elderly start with half doses, the patient with sickle cell disease is believed and treated on time, and the patient who says ‘I can manage’ is asked whether she can breathe deeply and cough — that is the test of adequate analgesia, not stoicism.",
            "<strong>Patient-controlled analgesia</strong> lets the patient give herself a small intravenous dose of morphine (typically 1 mg) by pressing a button, with a <strong>lockout</strong> interval of about five minutes during which further presses give nothing, so that she matches the drug to her pain and a drowsy patient stops pressing before she overdoses. The safety depends on rules the nurse enforces: <strong>only the patient presses the button</strong> — never a relative, never a nurse — and she is taught this before the operation; the pump is programmed and checked by two nurses against the prescription; hourly <strong>respiratory rate, sedation score, pain score and oxygen saturation</strong>, the number of good and total demands and the total dose are recorded; supplementary oxygen runs at least for the first night; <strong>no other opioid</strong> is given by any route while the pump runs; the line has an anti-reflux valve so that morphine does not back up a fluid line and arrive as a bolus; <strong>naloxone</strong> is at hand; and a patient who is too confused, too young, too weak or unwilling to use the pump has a different regimen. A pump that is giving good analgesia is not stopped because a visitor thinks morphine is addictive — addiction from short-term postoperative opioids is vanishingly rare, and the nurse explains this.",
            "An <strong>epidural infusion</strong> of local anaesthetic with a low-dose opioid gives the best analgesia after major abdominal and thoracic surgery and allows deep breathing and early walking, and it carries its own observation chart: <strong>blood pressure and pulse</strong> (hypotension from the sympathetic block is treated with fluid and by calling the anaesthetist; the rate is turned down only on instruction), <strong>respiratory rate and sedation score</strong> hourly (the opioid can depress breathing late — up to 12 hours after a bolus), the <strong>sensory level</strong> of the block tested with ice and the <strong>motor block</strong> in the legs (a block that is climbing towards the chest, or new leg weakness after the block had been wearing off, is an emergency), the <strong>catheter site</strong> for leakage, swelling or redness and the dressing and filter intact, the <strong>line labelled</strong> and connected to nothing else, the <strong>pressure areas</strong> of a patient who cannot feel them, the <strong>bladder</strong>, the temperature, and the timing of <strong>low molecular weight heparin</strong> in relation to insertion and removal of the catheter (removed by trained staff at least 12 hours after a prophylactic dose and with the next dose delayed), because an epidural haematoma — new severe back pain with leg weakness or numbness — compresses the cord and is a surgical emergency. The patient mobilises with help, never alone, while the block is in.",
          ],
