            "<strong>Sepsis</strong> is infection with organ dysfunction, and after surgery its sources are the chest, the urine, the wound, a deep collection, an infected cannula or central line, an anastomotic leak and, in Ghana, malaria running alongside. The nurse suspects it when an infected or possibly infected patient develops <strong>new confusion or drowsiness</strong>, a <strong>respiratory rate of 22 or more</strong>, a <strong>systolic pressure of 100 or less</strong>, a falling urine output, a high or <strong>low</strong> temperature, mottled or cold skin, or an early warning score that is climbing — and <strong>septic shock</strong> when the pressure does not respond to fluid. The response is the <strong>Sepsis Six within one hour</strong>: <strong>three out</strong> — blood cultures (and wound, urine and sputum cultures) before the antibiotic, a lactate where it can be measured, and a catheter with hourly urine output — and <strong>three in</strong> — oxygen to keep the saturation at target, intravenous crystalloid fast (500 mL boluses to 30 mL/kg in an adult, reassessing), and <strong>broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics within the hour</strong>, not after the ward round; with a <strong>malaria test</strong> and antimalarials where it is positive, because the two are not alternatives. <strong>Source control</strong> follows — the abscess drained, the infected line removed and its tip cultured, the dead tissue debrided, the leaking anastomosis taken back to theatre — and the patient who needs vasopressors goes to intensive care. Every hour of delay in the antibiotic measurably increases the death rate; the nurse who recognises sepsis and starts the clock is the single most important person in the chain.",
            "Finally, the quieter complications: <strong>pressure injury</strong> that began on the operating table and shows on the ward, <strong>hypothermia</strong> that persists, <strong>hyperglycaemia</strong> in the stressed diabetic and the <strong>hypokalaemia</strong> of gut losses, <strong>malnutrition</strong> in the patient kept nil by mouth too long, pain that is undertreated and becomes chronic, and the <strong>psychological</strong> injuries — the shock of a stoma or a scar, fear, low mood, the nightmares of a patient who was aware under anaesthesia — all of which are prevented or caught by the ordinary care already described. The incisional hernia, adhesions and keloid belong to the follow-up clinic, and the examiner's point is the one with which this lesson began: on every postoperative day the nurse knows what she is looking for before she looks.",
